WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.919 --> 00:00:06.720
Edward Norton was deeply affected by the experience
of making American History X, partly due to

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.940
the film's dark subject matter, and partly
because the production was a very troubled

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:11.440
one.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.940
Here's why the actor was never quite the same
after making this film.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:21.200
In 1996, Norton's performance in Primal Fear
established him as a formidable talent, but

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:26.700
it was his starring role in the 1998's American
History X that truly showcased his talents.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.840
In the film, Norton plays Derek Vinyard, a
neo-Nazi who abandons his ideology after spending

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:32.980
some time in prison.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.820
He then tries to prevent his younger brother,
Danny, played by Edward Furlong, from following

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:39.160
in his footsteps.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.140
As Norton told GQ in 2019:

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.940
"The film was, like, even the poster was iconic.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:50.240
It was a Shakespearean type of a story by
design."

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:54.600
It's rare that an actor has to step in and
edit the film they're starring in, but that's

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.120
exactly what Edward Norton ended up doing.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:01.859
That's because the making of American History
X proved to be almost as controversial as

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.460
the film itself…and the film was plenty
controversial.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:06.580
"Come on, man!"

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.620
"Put it on the curb, right now!"

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:17.850
In the middle of the editing process, director
Tony Kaye had some serious creative differences

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:19.960
with the production company New Line Cinema.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:25.040
Kaye had previously worked as an advertising
director, and his experience in that industry

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.440
reportedly affected his editing decisions.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.880
In fact, David McKenna, the film's screenwriter,
told The Telegraph that:

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.960
"[Kaye] literally destroyed the movie.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:35.340
It was a commercial."

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:39.880
In the hopes of salvaging the film, Norton
stepped in and began working with Kaye, but

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.100
they were constantly at odds with one another.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:46.380
However, many of Norton's edits did wind up
in the final cut of the film.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:50.869
It was a new experience for Norton, but the
skill came in handy later: He ended up co-editing

00:01:50.869 --> 00:01:54.880
the 2005 neo-Western Down in the Valley, a
film he also starred in.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:00.140
After filming wrapped on American History
X, director Tony Kaye was very bitter towards

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:04.120
Edward Norton, and he wasn't shy about letting
the media know his thoughts.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.229
The Telegraph quotes him saying that,

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:11.360
"[Norton is] a narcissistic dilettante, obsessed
with his image, obsessed with screen time...I

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.660
was going to take Hollywood by storm, and
I would have but for that buffoon."

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.220
Keep in mind, Kaye doesn't seem particularly
modest himself.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.240
"I'm a storyteller.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:27.860
So, you know, making a television commercial
is telling a story of sorts."

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:32.440
Thanks to all the drama behind the scenes
during the filming of American History X,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:38.320
Norton earned a reputation for being "difficult"
to work with, but the actor rejects that characterization.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:39.780
As he told The Telegraph:

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.440
"Difficult is really a question of semantics.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:47.500
You can use that word or you can say rigorous,
committed, you can put it other ways.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.300
And I'll tell you a very true thing.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.240
None of what I would call the genuine talents
I've worked with, directors, have ever called

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:54.920
me difficult."

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.680
In a 2014 interview with Dan Rather, Edward
Norton confessed that,

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.100
"Making American History X was really hard.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.080
It was, it was a guerilla kind of experience."

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:09.260
Norton put in some serious hours at the gym
to convincingly play Derek.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:10.980
As Men's Workout reports,

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:14.879
"The actor gained 30 pounds of muscle in just
three months."

00:03:14.879 --> 00:03:16.660
The magazine quotes Norton as saying,

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:21.720
"I knew this guy was going to have to be really
physically fearsome and defined by rage…arming

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.980
himself against his own emotional pain, and
this body he’s created is the physical manifestation

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:27.480
of that."

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:32.140
After the film was released, people suddenly
perceived Norton as bigger, stronger, and

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:33.020
more intimidating.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.000
As he said on The Tim Ferriss Show in 2016,

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:44.260
"Doing that film created the strangest distortion
of perception on me."

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:45.640
He went on to say:

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.920
"It's unbelievable, the degree to which that
film and the magic of camera and art and black

00:03:49.939 --> 00:03:53.780
and white photography and all these things
made a lot of people think that I was a larger

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.280
and tougher person than I am."

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:59.360
It sounds like Edward Norton has never been
too concerned about box office returns.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:03.600
He'd much rather participate in films that
make a real cultural impact.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:08.540
To hear him say it, American History X is
a film that really managed to capture the

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.720
zeitgeist, and he's proud to have been a part
of it.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.060
In 2010, he told Slash Film:

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:18.130
"A lot of films are more successful financially
than Fight Club or American History X, but

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.540
those films meant more to people and continue
to.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:25.020
To me, if I can make a couple of films that,
looking back, people say, 'Yeah, that’s

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:27.220
what our generation felt like at that moment.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:30.020
That’s a good document,' then I’m content."

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:36.060
In a 2016 interview with BET, Norton was asked
whether he thinks the issue of racism has

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:38.840
changed since making the film in 1998.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.200
He responded:

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:48.720
"To a degree we're not always comfortable
acknowledging this current of racism sits

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:49.820
under the surface."

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:55.190
He added that anxiety is often the cause of
those feelings, saying people, quote, "have

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:56.580
anxieties and they look for a target."

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:02.000
Derek goes through quite a dramatic personal
transformation in American History X, and

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.060
that journey is one of the things that really
excited Edward Norton about the character.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.620
As he told Charlie Rose in 1998:

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:13.920
"That's the challenge of a role like that,
or the appeal of it for me."

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.380
He goes on to explain that,

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:19.960
"[It's] not even so much just the one very
extreme manifestation of this guy, but more

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:22.020
the emotional distance that he travels."

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:26.300
But Norton had to do more than convincingly
portray Derek's hatred.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:31.240
He also had to create a nuanced character
who could learn how to become a more open-minded

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:33.100
person throughout the course of the film.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:37.620
Ultimately, Norton interpreted Derek Vinyard
as a classically tragic figure:

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:45.160
"Let's look at someone who ruins all of the
potential in his life because of rage."

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:50.630
Norton also wanted audiences to see something
of themselves in Vinyard, despite the extreme,

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:52.980
and extremely ugly, nature of his character.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.610
In 1999, he told The Guardian:

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:00.660
"An all-too-common reaction to something like
racism is to hate the act so much you dismiss

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:01.180
the person.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:05.820
But in this film you're forced to confront
the complexity of the character and his tragedy."

